  "full_response": "Observability in software systems refers to the ability to infer the internal state of a system solely from its external outputs. It is a concept that encompasses various methods and tools used to measure and understand the behavior of a distributed system, focusing on comprehending its dynamic state and diagnosing issues primarily through indirect data collection. Key components of observability include logs, metrics, and traces:\n\n1. **Logs**: Records of events or transactions that provide a time-sequenced, detailed account of activities within the system.\n2. **Metrics**: Numeric data that can be measured over time, providing a quantitative view of system performance (e.g., CPU usage, response time).\n3. **Traces**: Detailed records of requests as they flow through the system, capturing the path and duration of each request to understand latency and bottlenecks.\n\nObservability is often associated with the \"three pillars\" of observability: metrics for providing a high-level view of system health, logs for detailed data when issues arise, and traces to follow detailed request paths, enabling developers to understand where and why bottlenecks and failures occur.\n\nIn contrast, monitoring encompasses strategies and technologies used to continuously collect, analyze, and report on metrics from systems and applications to ensure they are operating correctly. It typically involves the following:\n\n- **Alerting**: Using predefined rules to trigger alerts when performance deviates from expected behaviors (e.g., response time threshold breaches).\n- **Dashboards**: Visual interfaces that aggregate metrics and logs to provide a real-time view of system health and performance.\n- **Performance Tracking**: Focusing on numbers and KPIs that represent the health of the system.\n\n### Key Differences Between Observability and Monitoring:\n\n- **Scope**:\n  - **Monitoring** involves the proactive collection of metrics and logs to assess the system's current state against expected performance baselines.\n  - **Observability** includes monitoring but emphasizes post-mortem analysis and understanding the internal workings of a system from external outputs.\n\n- **Objective**:\n  - **Monitoring** aims for early detection of issues and often prevents problems by alerting operators to anomalies.\n  - **Observability** aims to provide deep insights into the internal state, helping diagnose and understand complex system behaviors after issues have occurred.\n\n- **Focus**:\n  - **Monitoring** is often rule-based and focuses on specific metrics, events, and predefined alerts.\n  - **Observability** relies on data-driven insights, focusing on a comprehensive understanding of how the system operates as a whole.\n\n- **Approach**:\n  - **Monitoring",
